StringBuilder (Mutable & Not Thread-Safe)
a. Defined in java.lang.StringBuilder
b. Mutable (like StringBuffer)
c. Not thread-safe (faster in single-threaded environments)
d. Better performance than StringBuffer when synchronization is not needed
Example:
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
sb.append(" World");
System.out.println(sb);  // Output: Hello World



Methods of String Builder Class

A. Modification Methods
1) append(String str) – Adds the given string to the end.

       StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
       sb.append(" World");  // Hello World

2) insert(int offset, String str) – Inserts the string at the specified position.

       sb.insert(5, " Java");  // Hello Java World

3) replace(int start, int end, String str) – Replaces characters between start and end.

       sb.replace(6, 10, "Python");  // Hello Python World

4) delete(int start, int end) – Deletes characters between start and end.

       sb.delete(5, 12);  // Hello World

5) deleteCharAt(int index) – Deletes the character at the specified index.

       sb.deleteCharAt(2);  // Heo World
       
6) reverse() – Reverses the string.

       sb.reverse();  // dlroW olleH
       
       
B. Information & Utility Methods
1)length() – Returns the number of characters in the StringBuilder.

       int len = sb.length();

2) capacity() – Returns the current capacity of the StringBuilder object.

       int cap = sb.capacity();
       
3) charAt(int index) – Returns the character at the given position.

       char ch = sb.charAt(3);  // 'l'
       
4) setCharAt(int index, char ch) – Changes a character at the specified position.
 
       sb.setCharAt(2, 'X');  // HeXlo World

5) substring(int start) – Returns the substring from the specified start index.

       String sub = sb.substring(5);  // "World"

6) substring(int start, int end) – Returns the substring between start and end - 1.
 
       String sub = sb.substring(3, 7);  // "lo W"
       
     
C. Conversion Method
1) toString() – Converts StringBuilder into a String.

       String str = sb.toString();
